THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL - copywriting

This directory is somebody else's work, bundled into RichOS. It is not
RichOS-authored and it is not covered by this repository's AGPL.

  Source     https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills
  Skill      skills/copywriting
  Revision   68f5eaf64e858438db47e436d7a3bef0e9d69721 (2026-03-04)
  Terms      MIT, from the upstream LICENSE file at that revision, reproduced
             verbatim below

VERIFIED 2026-09-04 against that revision. SKILL.md (7,533 bytes),
evals/evals.json (8,158 bytes) and references/copy-frameworks.md (7,887 bytes)
are byte-identical to upstream.

ONE FILE IS MODIFIED, and this is the notice of it.
references/natural-transitions.md differs from upstream in four lines, and in
nothing but spelling. Two headings - "Emphasizing Key Points" and "Summarizing
Sections" - carry the British -ising form upstream and the American -izing form
here, and each appears twice, once in the contents list and once in the heading
it points at. That is the whole difference: four lines, two words, no change of
meaning and no change of guidance.

The cause is not guessed; it is in this repository's own history, at commit
06f4a8221a61500b90790383c8359d931391fe4f (2026-08-30), "docs(prose): US
spelling in engine/scripts, engine/skills and root repo comments". That sweep
enforces American spelling across the tree and cannot tell a vendored
third-party file from one we wrote.

MIT does not require a modified copy to say so. This says so anyway, because
docs/legal/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md must not call this skill byte-identical when
it is not, and because a future byte comparison against upstream would
otherwise look like a mystery.

The four spellings were left as they are rather than reverted. Silently editing
third-party content to make a provenance claim come true is the failure this
notice exists to prevent; whoever owns the skill library can revert them with
the fact in hand.

